To make the circle element appear more 3D in CSS, you can add some depth and shadows using the following CSS properties:

1. Perspective: This property adds a 3D effect by creating a virtual viewing point. Set this value for the parent container (#chart-area) to give the circle a sense of depth.

   ```css
   #chart-area {
     perspective: 1000px;
   }
   ```

2. Transform: Use the translateZ() function to move the circle forward or backward in the z-axis. The bigger the value, the farther away it appears. Negative values bring the circle closer to the viewer.

   ```css
   .circle {
     transform: translateZ(10px);
   }
   ```

3. Box-shadow: Add a shadow to the circle to create the illusion of depth. Use a blur radius, spread radius, and color to adjust the appearance of the shadow.

   ```css
   .circle {
     box-shadow: 0px 0px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5), 0px 0px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
   }
   ```

4. Background: Use a linear gradient to add texture and depth to the circle. The top color should be white, and the bottom color should be a lighter shade of the same color.

   ```css
   .circle {
     background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
   }
   ```

