```

The above function implements the classic bubble sort algorithm which iterates through the list multiple times comparing adjacent elements and swapping them if they are in the wrong order until the entire list is sorted. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n^2). However, it has the advantage of being simple and easy to understand, making it a great choice for educational purposes or small lists with few elements.

Note that there are more efficient sorting algorithms such as merge sort, quicksort, and heap sort available in the `sorted()` built-in function or external libraries like NumPy. These methods offer better performance and scalability for larger input sizes but may have steeper learning curves or require additional imports. You can choose the best one based on your specific use case and preferences.

