To implement this functionality in Go, we can follow these steps:

1. Define a list of common negative words in Go. We will store them in a map for easy lookup.

```go
negatives := map[string]struct{}{
    "n't": {},
    "no": {},
    "nor": {},
    "not": {},
}
```

2. Write a function to check if a sentence contains a double negative.

```go
func containsDoubleNegation(sentence string) bool {
    // Split the sentence into words
    words := strings.Split(sentence, " ")

    // Initialize variables to keep track of negations
    currentNegation := false
    lastWordWasNegated := false

    // Iterate over each word
    for _, word := range words {
        // Check if the current word is a negative word
        if _, found := negatives[word]; found {
            // If we haven't already marked this word as negated,
            // set the flag and continue
            if !lastWordWasNegated {
                currentNegation = true
            }
        } else {
            // If we've already marked a previous word as negated,
            // and this word isn't negated, we have a double negative
            if currentNegation && !lastWordWasNegated {
                return true
            }
            // Otherwise, reset our flags
            currentNegation = false
            lastWordWasNegated = false
        }
        // Update the flag for the previous word being negated
        lastWordWasNegated = currentNegation
    }
    // At the end, if we still have a negation flag set,
    // there's a double negative at the end of the sentence
    return currentNegation || lastWordWasNegated
}
```

